# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Used by docker-compose (env_file).

# --- Planning Center Personal Access Token (required) ---
PCO_APP_ID=your_pat_application_id
PCO_SECRET=your_pat_secret

# --- optional ---
# Pin the API version (defaults to the spec this build targets).
PCO_API_VERSION=2026-06-04
# Identify yourself to PCO (required header; put a real contact).
PCO_USER_AGENT=pcomirror/0.1 (+admin@yourchurch.org)

# Public HTTPS URL of THIS service, used when registering webhooks. PCO must be
# able to reach it (put a reverse proxy / tunnel in front that terminates TLS and
# forwards to the container's :8080). Example:
PCOMIRROR_PUBLIC_URL=https://pcomirror.yourchurch.org

# Auto-run an initial backfill on first `serve` startup (1 = yes). At ~300 people
# this is a few requests; leave unset to backfill manually instead.
PCOMIRROR_BACKFILL_ON_START=0

# Webhook subscriptions, re-applied on every `serve` start so a container needs no
# follow-up command. Comma-separated:
#   <subscription_id>:<event>:<url_token>:<authenticity_secret>
# You choose <url_token>, so the receiver URL — PCOMIRROR_PUBLIC_URL + /pco/webhooks/<token>
# — is known before first boot: register it at PCO, then paste PCO's secret here.
# Idempotent (keyed on subscription id); leave the token field empty to keep an
# existing token or mint a random one. Use the JSON form if a secret contains a ','
#   [{"id":"sub_123","event":"...","token":"person-updated-01","secret":"whsec_aaa"}]
#
# Several entries may share one <url_token>: Planning Center makes one
# subscription per event name but lets them all point at one URL, which is what
# ticking a column of events in its console does. The receiver works out which
# subscription a delivery came from by the secret that signed it, so mixed
# secrets on one URL are fine.
#
# Leaving the secret EMPTY (sub_123:people.v2.events.person.updated::) turns the
# signature check off for that subscription, moving the authentication into the
# URL. Leave the TOKEN empty too and a 32-character random one is minted, which
# makes the URL a bearer credential — a normal security model, and the mirror
# stays quiet about it. Type a short or memorable token AND leave the secret off
# and nothing authenticates a delivery: `serve` names that receiver at every
# start, and the admin page banners it.
#
# This is the DEFAULT, not the last word: /admin/webhooks manages the same list
# and wins once you save anything there, so a restart cannot undo a webhook fixed
# from the page. `serve` says so in its log; the page hands control back.
# PCOMIRROR_SUBSCRIPTIONS=sub_123:people.v2.events.person.updated:person-events-01:whsec_aaa

# The webhooks app's base URL, used only by the admin page's "refresh the event
# list from Planning Center" button. Empty = derived from PCO_BASE_URL, so
# pointing that at a stand-in moves both together.
# PCO_WEBHOOKS_BASE_URL=https://api.planningcenteronline.com/webhooks/v2

# Serve /people/v2 with NO API key (1 = yes). Only for a trusted LAN — anyone who
# can reach the port gets every mirrored person, and can write through to PCO with
# your PAT. Leave unset and mint keys instead:
#   pcomirror create-api-key --name <app> --scopes 'read:*'
# PCOMIRROR_ALLOW_ANONYMOUS=0

# --- Browser access (CORS) ---
# Which page origins may read /people/v2 from a browser. Unset = off, and off is
# silent: no CORS headers at all and OPTIONS answers 405, which is what a browser
# needs to see to conclude the service is not for it. There is no permissive
# default on purpose — this is a mirror of a church's people database.
#
# These are the DEFAULT, not the last word: /admin/cors manages the same policy
# and wins the moment anything is saved there, applying to the next request with
# no restart — so a browser app fixed at 9pm stays fixed when the container comes
# back at 3am. `serve` prints which source is in force; the page hands it back.
#
# Comma-separated origins, exactly as a browser sends them: scheme://host[:port],
# no trailing slash (a malformed entry fails startup rather than never matching).
# A leading *. wildcards the first label, so https://*.church.org allows
# app.church.org and a.b.church.org but NOT church.org itself. '*' allows any
# origin — then any page in any browser may use this API with any key it holds.
# 'null' (file:// pages, sandboxed iframes) is opt-in and is barely a boundary.
# PCOMIRROR_CORS_ORIGINS=https://directory.yourchurch.org,https://*.yourchurch.org

# Which methods those origins may use. Default: all four the service serves.
# Set GET for browser apps that only read — writes proxy to PCO with your PAT.
# PCOMIRROR_CORS_METHODS=GET,POST,PATCH,DELETE

# Request headers a page may send (default Authorization,Content-Type — the API
# key and a JSON:API body) and response headers it may read (default
# X-Mirror-Source,Location: mirror-or-pass-through, and where a 201 put the new
# record). '*' is allowed in either, but not together with credentials below.
# Cache-Control, Pragma, Expires, If-None-Match and If-Modified-Since are allowed
# whatever this lists: an HTTP client library adds them for its own caching, they
# carry no authority, and refusing one fails a request the app never decorated.
# PCOMIRROR_CORS_HEADERS=Authorization,Content-Type
# PCOMIRROR_CORS_EXPOSE_HEADERS=X-Mirror-Source,Location

# How long a browser may cache the preflight answer, in seconds (0 = not at all).
# PCOMIRROR_CORS_MAX_AGE=600

# Let cross-origin requests carry cookies / browser-remembered Basic credentials.
# Off, and rarely wanted: an API key travels in the Authorization header, which
# needs none of this. Cannot be combined with '*' anywhere — browsers reject that
# combination, so startup does too.
# PCOMIRROR_CORS_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS=0
#
# Note what this never covers, from here or from the page: the operator console
# (/ and /admin/**) and the webhook receiver are never cross-origin readable.
# An API key in browser JavaScript is also readable by anyone who opens the page.

# How often to run the full-id delete audit, in hours (0 switches it off). It is
# the only mechanism that finds a record hard-deleted at PCO with no webhook and
# no merge — nothing else ever looks, because `where[updated_at]` cannot return
# an id that no longer exists.
# PCOMIRROR_AUDIT_INTERVAL_HOURS=24

# How many diagnostic events to keep (every mutation, and every upstream failure
# including ones a retry recovered from). Read them at /admin/diagnostics.
# 0 switches recording off.
# PCOMIRROR_DIAGNOSTIC_KEEP=1000

# Divergence checking: how many read *shapes* per scheduler pass to re-ask PCO
# about, comparing its answer with the mirror's. 0 (default) is off; it spends
# real PCO budget. Read the results at /admin/divergence.
# PCOMIRROR_SHADOW_PER_MINUTE=0
# How many reports to keep. A report holds two whole responses, so the log is
# also capped at 25 MB regardless of this number — whichever bound bites first
# drops the oldest reports.
# PCOMIRROR_SHADOW_KEEP=200

# How many calls to the webhook receiver to record. Every one is kept verbatim —
# the method, the path, every header including the authenticity signature, and
# the exact request bytes — the deliveries that were refused as much as the ones
# that were kept, because a 401, a 404 or an unparseable body otherwise leaves no
# trace of what arrived. Read or download them at /admin/webhooks/calls, where
# the number can also be changed without a restart. 0 switches recording off.
#
# Nothing is redacted, so the table and its download hold whole webhook payloads:
# names, addresses, phone numbers. Handle the file like the database.
# PCOMIRROR_WEBHOOK_RECORD_KEEP=500

# Run the service as this uid/gid (default 10001:10001). Set these to the host
# user that owns a bind-mounted /data — otherwise SQLite reports "unable to open
# database file". On a Synology, `id <your-user>` over SSH gives the numbers.
# Ignored when the container is started with --user.
# PUID=1026
# PGID=100

# TLS: path (inside the container) to a CA bundle if PCO egress goes via a proxy.
# PCO_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt

# The DB path and bind host/port already have container-friendly defaults
# (/data/pcomirror.db, 0.0.0.0:8080) — override only if you need to.
# PCOMIRROR_DB=/data/pcomirror.db
# PCOMIRROR_PORT=8080
